findings.description:
Tiers 2–5 are all defined as "Quadrants," and Overview calls GPU migration "required." Yet the "Quadrants substrate evaluation" slice has no go/no-go criteria, and no alternatives are named. The doc itself calls Quadrants "a young fork… limited community history." The mitigation ("Numba tier provides a working fallback at 10k") only protects Tier 1. If Quadrants fails on Metal or at scale, nothing in the doc reaches 50k+. The doc needs:
- explicit evaluation criteria: Metal kernel perf versus Numba, availability of sort and prefix-sum primitives on Metal, and kNN feasibility;
- the alternatives considered (upstream Taichi, other GPU array or kernel frameworks);
- a plan B for Tiers 2+.

The evaluation should also run before 118, not after. If Quadrants' CPU backend matches Numba, the Numba tier becomes redundant (see the next finding).

"Port all BulkForce implementations to `@ti.kernel`" targets a moving set. Forces 103 (predator-prey), 104 (territorial), 115 (interaction matrix), and 107 are still planned, and 114's `PropulsionForce` exists. Each force would need a NumPy reference, a Numba version, and a Quadrants version. The doc does not say:
- how the `force_builder` registry selects an implementation per backend;
- whether a force that lacks a GPU version is allowed in a GPU tick (which would force a device→host round trip mid-tick);
- how profile-interaction lookup tables (the index-based dispatch in 100-arch) reach the kernels.

Quadrants already has a CPU backend on ARM64 and x86, so a single-source Quadrants kernel could serve Tier 1 as well. The Numba path (118) might then only need to be a test oracle, or be dropped. Decide this before committing to three parallel code paths.
